AI Satellite Collision Prevention System — a story

Picture low Earth orbit as the world's worst highway. No lanes, no traffic lights, no rearview mirrors, and everyone's doing 28,000 km/h. Over 10,000 cars are on this highway right now, none of them can brake, most of them can barely steer, and every single one is running on a gas tank the size of an energy drink — a gas tank that has to last for YEARS. Also: if any two of them so much as kiss bumpers, they don't just crumple a fender. They explode into a few thousand bullets and hand those bullets to everyone else on the road, forever, or until Earth's thin outer atmosphere gets around to sweeping up the mess (decades, if it's feeling generous).

This is the actual problem satellite operators face every day. It is somehow not a bigger meme.

So we built the thing that should exist for this: an AI system that watches the whole highway, figures out which cars are actually on a collision course (not just "kind of close," but mathematically, provably, going-to-hit-each-other close), and decides what to do about it before it becomes a very expensive fireworks show.

Here's how the story goes, module by module:

Chapter 1, "Falling With Style" (orbital_mechanics.py) — Every satellite is just... falling. Constantly. It's just falling sideways fast enough to keep missing the ground, which is the entire secret of orbit and also the entire plot of a certain movie about a toy astronaut. We simulate that fall precisely, including the annoying real-world stuff that messes with clean textbook ellipses: Earth's equatorial bulge tugging things sideways, wisps of atmosphere at 400km quietly sanding down orbits, and sunlight literally shoving on spacecraft like a very slow-motion cosmic bully. We also track how fuzzy our knowledge of "where exactly is this thing" gets over time, because tracking stations aren't psychic.

Chapter 2, "Needle, Meet Haystack" (conjunction.py) — With 10,000 objects, there are ~50 million possible ways for two of them to meet. Checking all of them properly would take forever, so we do what any sane bouncer does: cheap filters first. "Do these two even use the same altitude?" cuts out most of the crowd immediately. What's left gets the real math — the actual probability-of-collision integral, the same one real operators use, not a vibes-based guess. If the odds cross about 1-in-10,000, that's the system's version of a raised eyebrow.

Chapter 3, "The Tiny Nudge That Saves the Day" (avoidance.py) — Dodging in space isn't like dodging a dodgeball. You can't juke sideways. You nudge your velocity a tiny bit, and the universe slowly, patiently turns that tiny nudge into a big miss — but only if you do it early. A whisper of a burn a full orbit ahead of time beats a desperate shove at the last second, every time. Problem is, most satellites have about 25 m/s of fuel budget for their ENTIRE LIFE. That's not a gas tank, that's a shot glass. So the system is stingy on purpose: maneuver only when the math says it's actually worth the sip.

Chapter 4, "Plan B, When Plan A Fails" (damage_minimization.py) — Sometimes you can't dodge. Maybe the warning came too late, maybe the fuel's gone. When that happens, the system doesn't just shrug — it runs the NASA breakup model (yes, a real model, born from blowing things up on purpose in labs) to figure out how to make the crash suck less: turn edge-on instead of flat-on, aim for a glancing sideswipe instead of a head-on hit, or bias the wreck toward a lower altitude where atmospheric drag will actually clean up after the mess in months instead of centuries. Damage control, spacecraft edition.

Chapter 5, "The Chess Master" (risk_optimizer.py) — Here's the twist: it's never just one collision to worry about. It's hundreds, all at once, all sharing the same limited fuel, all tangled together — dodge one thing and you might steer straight into another. So the system treats the whole constellation like a social network of risk, finds the tightly-connected clusters, and figures out which single maneuver quietly defuses an entire cluster at once. For the gnarliest cases, it borrows the exact algorithm that beat humans at Go (Monte Carlo Tree Search) to explore "if I do this, then that happens, then..." possibilities several moves deep. It's chess, except the pieces are worth billions of dollars and moving too slow means Kessler syndrome — the orbital equivalent of a car crash that causes more car crashes, forever.

Chapter 6, "Show, Don't Just Tell" (the dashboard) — All of this gets rendered live: 3D orbit views, risk timelines, debris breakdowns, an LLM narrator translating the raw numbers into something a human operator can actually read on a bad day without a physics degree.

The moral of the story: fuel is finite, threats never stop showing up, and every decision you make now quietly taxes every decision you'll get to make later. That's not a physics problem anymore — that's a strategy problem. And strategy problems are exactly what this system was built to solve, one very small, very well-timed nudge at a time.

The end. (Or, if we do our job right, no ending at all — just a very boring, very uneventful highway.)
